#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221091 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fee186e0-579f-5104-b0e1-50540abc0bdc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "35a158c3-37b6-5123-ba90-1fd05218ce43" :question/math-academy-id "e-21271" :question/problem "Find $e^{At}$ for ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/assets/bf972d6172dc5282be366a87138ac9088216484082251482383de605c720b568.png) \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $1$ and $4.$*" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=4t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ r(4t)={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ 4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3t\\alpha_1 = e^{4t} - e^{t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_1 = \\dfrac{1}{3t}\\bigl(e^{4t}-e^{t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $4$ and subtracting the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3\\alpha_0 = 4e^{t} - e^{4t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_0 = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigl(4e^{t}-e^{4t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}+4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t} \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-3(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-{e}^{4t} \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((4{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{t})+(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t})) \\\\  & ={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/assets/104a4830d44821dbc65ad9c832da0323b7dd9e90c934fdfc7a21c175845231d2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221092 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a118fe4a-cd1f-52f7-aa33-59f5494e4905" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "18064c11-63d3-599a-b504-7b589f46c3dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21272" :question/problem "Find $e^{At}$ for ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/assets/3f97cbbd4715d8191dc5b0c410a784b556b068ed5e3e57fe0dc16ee8f52655cc.png) \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $3 \\pm 2\\textrm{i}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1= (3+2\\textrm{i}) t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2= (3-2\\textrm{i}) t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((3+2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(3+2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(3+2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ (3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+2{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)), \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/assets/dec5e32e503bf178832f6cf08b51767d2f76fb308e9a9d07c4e805389507bbfd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221093 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fd1d280-c768-5504-892e-a9a1948e1f1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "40a6318a-a1c9-55f3-afdb-02b43891d7b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-21273" :question/problem "Find $e^{At}$ for ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/assets/9657b1346f9aa79eaa2d05d0a94b5e13745fedc83b035cbd59f1efdb10ce19bf.png) \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $6,$ and it has multiplicity two.*" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 6t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(6t)={e}^{6t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(6t)={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}6t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 = e^{6t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n6t e^{6t} + \\alpha_0 = e^{6t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_0 = e^{6t}(1-6t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =8{e}^{6t}t+{e}^{6t}(1-6t) \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(1+2t), \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-2{e}^{6t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(-2t), \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2{e}^{6t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(2t), \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4{e}^{6t}t+{e}^{6t}(1-6t) \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/assets/70c4e625ac52b04e9a1fb258470138050a51cdacaced8714cd2a313782055c3f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221135 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4fc3ba4-d488-5156-88dc-837369269cef" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Matrix Exponential"}] :question/id #uuid "c0213c76-94e8-5ab3-a260-8a78a205589a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21724" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/assets/f97a3d1392b000bb1f02f0f8cd567b72d1a6792e4bc9da41ea24cc4ebbc7208f.png)\nGiven the matrices $A$ and $e^{At}$ above, compute $\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t} \\big(e^{At}\\big).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t} \\big(e^{At}\\big) = A \\, e^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & t{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & (t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fee186e0-579f-5104-b0e1-50540abc0bdc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5f8d9bb-68c3-5575-85ee-59e1bb852a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e^{-4t}-2e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336649} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bdb272-a5e4-5e25-b31e-cdd810dbb3cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e^{-4t}+2e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5f8d9bb-68c3-5575-85ee-59e1bb852a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e^{-4t}-2e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336649} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1a154c5-434e-5383-ba15-655422aebb15" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220076} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3784cf1a-49af-59de-9580-29afb8cadba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{-4t}-2e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3784cf1a-49af-59de-9580-29afb8cadba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{-4t}-2e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336651} :answer-field/id #uuid "26b496f1-86a3-585d-8d51-7626b5ce0be7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cdf86a0b-ce7f-5a36-9106-b67d70a6ba24" :question/math-academy-id "q-323878" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-4$ and $1.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,\\frac{1}{5}$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-7{e}^{-4t}+7{e}^{t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $-2{e}^{-4t}+7{e}^{t}$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-4t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-4t)={e}^{-4t} \\\\ r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t} \\\\ t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtraction the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-5t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second equation by $4$ and adding to the first one, we get\n\n$$\n5{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =6(-\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-6{e}^{-4t}+6{e}^{t}))+({e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(7{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t}) \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-2(-\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t} & -7{e}^{-4t}+7{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t} & -2{e}^{-4t}+7{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fee186e0-579f-5104-b0e1-50540abc0bdc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "506e6999-249f-590e-a21f-830d2aff622e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+3e^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "506e6999-249f-590e-a21f-830d2aff622e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+3e^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336654} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eef5b3f-6f55-58be-a7e6-f1cd31bfe1e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336653} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5259626-7126-58ad-a1a2-94f3af0bfe00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-e^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5259626-7126-58ad-a1a2-94f3af0bfe00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-e^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336656} :answer-field/id #uuid "786edaef-0867-5181-a748-c68cb87d67bc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8324ba89-f95b-5f1e-b38a-6e5690cd5349" :question/math-academy-id "q-333918" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-4$ and $0.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-3+3{e}^{-4t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $3-{e}^{-4t}$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-4t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-4t)={e}^{-4t} \\\\ r(0)=1\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Substituting ${\\alpha }_{0}=1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+1={e}^{-4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-6(-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-1))t+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4}({e}^{-4t}-1)+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}({e}^{-4t}-1)+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3+3{e}^{-4t}+2}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+3{e}^{-4t}}{2} \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2(-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-1))t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4}({e}^{-4t}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-4t}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-4t}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1+3{e}^{-4t} & -3+3{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-4t} & 3-{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fee186e0-579f-5104-b0e1-50540abc0bdc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63edc7a7-e202-5140-ad15-e3bdd648c8fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^t-e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336659} {:answer/id #uuid "cb352320-6485-5239-9876-6cd98b95e257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^t+e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63edc7a7-e202-5140-ad15-e3bdd648c8fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^t-e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336659} :answer-field/id #uuid "11a13db0-f8a7-5321-a805-db5e38905f97" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336658} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65c231c1-8543-5666-85d6-3726ca4aff5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^t-e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65c231c1-8543-5666-85d6-3726ca4aff5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^t-e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336662} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec410e9d-1659-51ee-8cf3-e0353d1699f0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bdb6f424-6e44-59e6-9a93-01c56b71efc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-333921" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-2$ and $1.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,$ |  | $-{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t}$ | $-2{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Explanation:\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-2t)={e}^{-2t} \\\\ r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t} \\\\ t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-3t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding to the first one, we get\n\n$$\n3{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3(-\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & =-({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((-4{e}^{-2t}+4{e}^{t})+({e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t})) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t} & -2{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fee186e0-579f-5104-b0e1-50540abc0bdc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "350942f2-1b65-50b6-8378-f520b4571b5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4t}-e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "350942f2-1b65-50b6-8378-f520b4571b5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4t}-e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336665} :answer-field/id #uuid "15aae7e9-3199-5dbc-91df-c1f4094dc897" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336664} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71f9e1ab-5a83-5490-980e-4996871e0465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{4t}+e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71f9e1ab-5a83-5490-980e-4996871e0465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{4t}+e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336667} :answer-field/id #uuid "45e929a3-252b-5d53-9706-b775659c76bb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a46eaf89-1caa-5e37-8736-f496ce5e89c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-334581" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $1$ and $4.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ |  | ${e}^{4t}+2{e}^{t}$ | $2{e}^{4t}-2{e}^{t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ r(4t)={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ 4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $4$ and subtracting the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3{\\alpha }_{0}=4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}), \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =3(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((3{e}^{4t}-3{e}^{t})+(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2{e}^{4t}+{e}^{t}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t}+2{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{4t}-2{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}-{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{4t}+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fee186e0-579f-5104-b0e1-50540abc0bdc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "792f2907-2c81-51fe-9817-7242b18524b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "792f2907-2c81-51fe-9817-7242b18524b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336670} :answer-field/id #uuid "8af23d1c-5421-54f7-8aae-c58dd22ce93a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336669} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f36c42c2-f8ce-5ca0-a521-88f6a74ade84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f36c42c2-f8ce-5ca0-a521-88f6a74ade84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336672} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca354d56-da2d-5703-80f0-daa9a72009ed" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9704d1e-4934-59c8-af07-62fcf3c537b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-334586" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $0$ and $1.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $2-2{e}^{t}$ | $2{e}^{t}-1$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=0,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(0)={e}^{0} \\\\ r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=1 \\\\ t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the first equation, we immediately get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Substituting ${\\alpha }_{0}=1$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt{\\alpha }_{1}+1={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{t}({e}^{t}-1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-(\\frac{1}{t}({e}^{t}-1))t+1 \\\\  & =-({e}^{t}-1)+1 \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}+2 \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & =(\\frac{1}{t}({e}^{t}-1))t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{t}+2 & {e}^{t}-1 \\\\ 2-2{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a118fe4a-cd1f-52f7-aa33-59f5494e4905" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe9f1aef-6f4d-57f4-a491-5b6a298f56ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe9f1aef-6f4d-57f4-a491-5b6a298f56ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336675} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ffdc540-ceb1-5578-af7f-bbf66d74c6d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336674} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa770650-4c47-5ffb-88f8-6e5ab567232c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(4t)-\\sin(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa770650-4c47-5ffb-88f8-6e5ab567232c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(4t)-\\sin(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336677} :answer-field/id #uuid "25c7f3ad-370a-5db8-b49e-c97c86f9fb23" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "523c3856-6547-5ca9-bf3b-0dee4130679b" :question/math-academy-id "q-334629" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $1\\pm 4\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ | $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 8{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -4{\\alpha }_{1}t & -3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(1+4\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(1-4\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((1+4\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(1+4\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(1+4\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ ({\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+4{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 4t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{4t}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{4t}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot t+{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ -4{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4t}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a118fe4a-cd1f-52f7-aa33-59f5494e4905" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac3b95f2-1a28-5119-a61c-061d3f770dfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(2t)-\\sin(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac3b95f2-1a28-5119-a61c-061d3f770dfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(2t)-\\sin(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336680} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ec8784d-6204-5f00-b13e-8480c385bcdf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336679} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2661c747-5fc3-5f13-ad50-6a8287c7cc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(2t)+\\sin(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2661c747-5fc3-5f13-ad50-6a8287c7cc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(2t)+\\sin(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336682} :answer-field/id #uuid "40727877-1b7c-5122-b4ff-1cf330a55013" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dcc5260c-edf1-576a-a819-02c8686a34fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-334630" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{-t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(-1+2\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(-1-2\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((-1+2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(-1+2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(-1+2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ (-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+2{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a118fe4a-cd1f-52f7-aa33-59f5494e4905" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ac60207-92e5-5e25-8288-51c01431fe1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(5t)-\\sin(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336685} {:answer/id #uuid "09caff92-8dfb-5ea4-8b72-56b92908a112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(5t)+\\sin(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ac60207-92e5-5e25-8288-51c01431fe1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(5t)-\\sin(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336685} :answer-field/id #uuid "a159a006-28db-5c40-b783-5dd57f7e0b08" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336684} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8017c757-8f0f-5c58-8f81-593b73010455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8017c757-8f0f-5c58-8f81-593b73010455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336688} :answer-field/id #uuid "2846a1a7-73eb-5f75-bb7e-249d41f8f017" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65344152-712e-5f35-b3c9-d55e11563216" :question/math-academy-id "q-334631" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $2\\pm 5\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -10{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(2+5\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(2-5\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((2+5\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(2+5\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(2+5\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ (2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+5{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{5t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t+{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a118fe4a-cd1f-52f7-aa33-59f5494e4905" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8fee772-3044-55ef-9e26-5cf3e50c193e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336691} {:answer/id #uuid "8a616c37-3f53-5149-bfa0-f8fa63183fca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a616c37-3f53-5149-bfa0-f8fa63183fca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336692} :answer-field/id #uuid "f43d4a8c-05ee-5960-82ed-bb9b1f6d54e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336690} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38920a45-beb5-59db-bcfd-5af86d116165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38920a45-beb5-59db-bcfd-5af86d116165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336694} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4e3ecaa-2a7d-5cbc-accd-8f4c4c20e6c5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e47fd713-a5e0-5600-9c23-a39a0b5a9832" :question/math-academy-id "q-334636" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\pm \\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,$ |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot \\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a118fe4a-cd1f-52f7-aa33-59f5494e4905" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94e49ff0-2dc3-59b9-af14-53e12a188425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13\\sin(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94e49ff0-2dc3-59b9-af14-53e12a188425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13\\sin(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336697} :answer-field/id #uuid "d97cf406-6502-5070-9bd0-2605e798079a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336696} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdd49fd6-c1be-5931-991b-0f01de2790ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cos(5t)-\\sin(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdd49fd6-c1be-5931-991b-0f01de2790ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cos(5t)-\\sin(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336699} :answer-field/id #uuid "4890c6c0-0a0c-5897-9c09-54f3b914a856" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd75718e-383f-5405-97b6-c584bfeba6cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-334637" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -13 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\pm 5\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\,$ |  | $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$ | $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -13 & -1\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -13{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-5\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(5\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{5\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot 5\\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+5{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-13{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-13\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fd1d280-c768-5504-892e-a9a1948e1f1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06bf0124-b1c2-5001-94b4-87e535d5e874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06bf0124-b1c2-5001-94b4-87e535d5e874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336702} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed929999-2f8d-5069-832f-748549b47078" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336701} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c92bf50-da2a-514e-852e-585ccc4a6179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c92bf50-da2a-514e-852e-585ccc4a6179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336704} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6f3aaab-d1ba-5f50-82e3-918845ce1930" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da6a0bee-e03f-5fcb-b30a-6ec5baf1b10b" :question/math-academy-id "q-334732" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $1,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | $1-3t$ | $t$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -9{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\nt{e}^{t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}(1-t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-9{e}^{t}t \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4{e}^{t}t+{e}^{t}(1-t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(1+3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1-3t & t \\\\ -9t & 1+3t\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fd1d280-c768-5504-892e-a9a1948e1f1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1650f1bb-a0f5-5945-9680-8e5b3efd86e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336707} {:answer/id #uuid "681e19b2-14b5-50e7-bbf9-ef51c3a13d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1650f1bb-a0f5-5945-9680-8e5b3efd86e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336707} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfaa8f70-1f62-5a45-b175-c4f69a486799" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336706} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c291dd-f550-5a52-9078-22538a48a8da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27c291dd-f550-5a52-9078-22538a48a8da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336710} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1fb94a4-d36e-58c2-9d9e-4049bb273284" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cfc425ce-a610-551a-a2b5-3dc42f05d117" :question/math-academy-id "q-334733" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $2,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-3t$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $1-6t$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 12{\\alpha }_{1}t & -4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=2t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(2t)={e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(2t)={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n2t{e}^{2t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}(1-2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =8{e}^{2t}t+{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(1+6t) \\\\ 12{\\alpha }_{1}t & =12{e}^{2t}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1+6t & -3t \\\\ 12t & 1-6t\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fd1d280-c768-5504-892e-a9a1948e1f1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74a8ff19-af59-5790-b218-d15841a03b9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336713} {:answer/id #uuid "1b6dd373-795a-5769-813d-0eb56367e442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b6dd373-795a-5769-813d-0eb56367e442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336714} :answer-field/id #uuid "921e925e-a5ad-5a8e-b040-0023fdbb432b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336712} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87f7b0fc-68a3-5034-8279-37184ae464a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87f7b0fc-68a3-5034-8279-37184ae464a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336716} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e5fa583-36a4-508d-aa53-2dcc6a0031bf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b49f596-6063-525c-9eb4-806d2f06952c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334734" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $6,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{6t}\\,\\,$ |  | $1+t$ | $t$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=6t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(6t)={e}^{6t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(6t)={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}6t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{6t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n6t{e}^{6t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t}(1-6t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-{e}^{6t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(-t), \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5{e}^{6t}t+{e}^{6t}(1-6t) \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(1-t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1+t & t \\\\ -t & 1-t\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fd1d280-c768-5504-892e-a9a1948e1f1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "524c9481-44d8-5a15-9669-009f31dbba3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "524c9481-44d8-5a15-9669-009f31dbba3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336719} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8bf27f2-3c96-5d62-9420-022489ab6c82" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336718} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f36d328f-e9e1-5ce3-9482-9c2edfcf7c48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f36d328f-e9e1-5ce3-9482-9c2edfcf7c48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336721} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dabeb5a-01c5-5ef9-88d4-a05f4eddcff0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd0c40bf-d79c-5896-86d7-85174a185df6" :question/math-academy-id "q-334735" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $3,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{3t}\\,\\,$ |  | $1+4t$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $8t$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -1\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 8{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(3t)={e}^{3t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n3t{e}^{3t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}(1-3t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-2{e}^{3t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(-2t) \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-{e}^{3t}t+{e}^{3t}(1-3t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(1-4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1+4t & -2t \\\\ 8t & 1-4t\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fd1d280-c768-5504-892e-a9a1948e1f1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35b251e5-2056-591e-aca5-2c4e081cdea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35b251e5-2056-591e-aca5-2c4e081cdea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336724} :answer-field/id #uuid "48f6ebe7-fa9d-50a8-94cf-b6b1f2d084dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336723} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7861317-d936-5791-a6e4-61e66dfed920" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7861317-d936-5791-a6e4-61e66dfed920" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336726} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e780bc0-6f34-5216-be99-ae10d43304f6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d958a3c1-9de6-5808-81d2-72a2e1f512c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-334737" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $1,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-5t$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $1-5t$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -5{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\nt{e}^{t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}(1-t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =6{e}^{t}t+{e}^{t}(1-t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(1+5t) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =5{e}^{t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1+5t & -5t \\\\ 5t & 1-5t\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4fc3ba4-d488-5156-88dc-837369269cef" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Matrix Exponential"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ce22ae-3828-5f35-be25-7cc431bae928" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336729} {:answer/id #uuid "8f199740-845c-515a-b21b-8d951918bd79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f199740-845c-515a-b21b-8d951918bd79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336730} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ff9c5c9-2296-5a8d-b4b8-9bd591066c38" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336728} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a2cddaf-1d9d-57f6-9698-382db86f84f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1+5t)e^{